1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:04,759
Every day food businesses have safe surplus
while nearby community

2
00:00:04,759 --> 00:00:09,664
groups are looking for food The hard part is not
finding a good intention

3
00:00:09,684 --> 00:00:13,969
It is coordinating the handoff keeping the
details clear and showing

4
00:00:13,969 --> 00:00:18,574
what actually happened A message can say that
food was available A

5
00:00:18,574 --> 00:00:22,426
spreadsheet can say that someone planned to
collect it Neither one

6
00:00:22,426 --> 00:00:27,082
gives everyone the same reliable timeline
RescueProof is built for

7
00:00:27,082 --> 00:00:29,013
that missing layer of trust

8
00:00:30,342 --> 00:00:34,718
RescueProof is designed around three people in
the same handoff A

9
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:38,315
food business needs to describe what is
available and when it must

10
00:00:38,315 --> 00:00:42,422
be collected An approved community organization
needs to claim the

11
00:00:42,422 --> 00:00:47,001
offer and receive the pickup details at the
right moment An operator

12
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:51,764
needs to see whether the process was completed
disputed or corrected

13
00:00:51,784 --> 00:00:55,968
Each person needs the same core facts without
exposing more private

14
00:00:55,968 --> 00:00:58,499
information than the workflow requires

15
00:00:59,842 --> 00:01:03,995
The product goal is practical Make the path from
surplus to receipt

16
00:01:03,995 --> 00:01:09,056
easy to follow The donor publishes the food
quantity pickup window

17
00:01:09,056 --> 00:01:13,934
storage requirements and allergen notes An
approved organization claims

18
00:01:13,934 --> 00:01:18,444
the listing The donor validates the handoff The
recipient confirms

19
00:01:18,444 --> 00:01:22,390
what was actually received This does not replace
an in-person food

20
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:26,556
safety check It creates a clear operational
record around that check

21
00:01:26,556 --> 00:01:29,774
so a coordinator can understand the full story
later

22
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:36,706
This is the complete workflow First a donor
publishes a listing Second

23
00:01:36,706 --> 00:01:41,620
an approved organization claims it Third both
sides confirm the handoff

24
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:45,799
and the actual received amount Finally a
reviewer can inspect the

25
00:01:45,799 --> 00:01:51,210
record and its integrity evidence Routine users
see a normal web application

26
00:01:51,216 --> 00:01:55,149
They do not need to understand wallets gas or
Merkle trees to share

27
00:01:55,149 --> 00:01:58,703
food The technical layer appears when a reviewer
needs independent

28
00:01:58,703 --> 00:01:59,580
evidence

29
00:02:00,842 --> 00:02:04,591
Here is the donor flow in the working prototype
The donor selects

30
00:02:04,591 --> 00:02:08,953
an organization names the food records the
quantity and estimated

31
00:02:08,953 --> 00:02:13,390
weight and sets the pickup window Storage
requirements and allergen

32
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:17,458
information are visible parts of the form not
hidden notes in a chat

33
00:02:17,458 --> 00:02:22,544
message The donor can review the details before
publishing This demonstration

34
00:02:22,544 --> 00:02:26,858
uses synthetic test data It does not represent a
real food offer or

35
00:02:26,858 --> 00:02:28,544
a real contact address

36
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:32,962
The recipient sees available food through a
separate organization

37
00:02:32,962 --> 00:02:36,870
account Claiming the listing creates an explicit
event and prevents

38
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:40,841
two groups from treating the same offer as
theirs Only after the claim

39
00:02:40,841 --> 00:02:44,787
succeeds do the private pickup details become
available This is a

40
00:02:44,787 --> 00:02:48,512
deliberate product choice Participants can use
the workflow without

41
00:02:48,512 --> 00:02:52,973
first creating a crypto wallet buying tokens or
paying a transaction

42
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:53,654
fee

43
00:02:54,925 --> 00:02:59,299
At handoff the recipient generates a time-
limited pickup code The

44
00:02:59,299 --> 00:03:03,037
donor checks that code before confirming the
collection Once it is

45
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:07,779
used the code is consumed and cannot authorize
another pickup This

46
00:03:07,779 --> 00:03:11,676
reduces a common coordination mistake the wrong
person arriving for

47
00:03:11,676 --> 00:03:16,058
the right listing or the same listing being
treated as open twice

48
00:03:16,064 --> 00:03:20,782
The code is only a workflow control It is not
proof of physical identity

49
00:03:20,782 --> 00:03:24,061
food safety or successful delivery by itself

50
00:03:25,342 --> 00:03:29,471
The donor confirms that the handoff happened The
recipient then enters

51
00:03:29,471 --> 00:03:33,797
the actual received weight and confirms receipt
In this synthetic

52
00:03:33,797 --> 00:03:38,907
test the recipient reports two point eight
kilograms RescueProof keeps

53
00:03:38,907 --> 00:03:42,926
that reported amount separate from the donor's
original estimate That

54
00:03:42,926 --> 00:03:46,774
distinction matters It prevents an estimate from
being presented as

55
00:03:46,774 --> 00:03:50,953
completed impact and it gives operators a way to
see where a correction

56
00:03:50,953 --> 00:03:52,725
or a dispute came from

57
00:03:54,008 --> 00:04:00,748
The client is Vue 3 and TypeScript The API is
Rust and Axum PostgreSQL

58
00:04:00,748 --> 00:04:06,250
is the source of truth for users organizations
batches claims handovers

59
00:04:06,250 --> 00:04:11,127
disputes and proof records Redis supports rate
limiting and short-lived

60
00:04:11,127 --> 00:04:15,723
workflow jobs Background workers process
notifications and evidence

61
00:04:15,723 --> 00:04:20,184
batching This separation keeps ordinary
operations responsive and

62
00:04:20,184 --> 00:04:24,522
gives the backend one place to enforce
authorization validation and

63
00:04:24,522 --> 00:04:26,183
transaction boundaries

64
00:04:27,425 --> 00:04:32,338
Web3 is used for one narrow purpose record
integrity Completed event

65
00:04:32,338 --> 00:04:36,640
payloads are hashed into a Merkle tree and a
compact root is anchored

66
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:41,162
on Ethereum Sepolia through a relayer The full
record pickup address

67
00:04:41,162 --> 00:04:45,574
and personal details stay off chain A reviewer
can compare the stored

68
00:04:45,574 --> 00:04:50,390
event its Merkle path and the public contract
root Putting every field

69
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:54,434
on chain would be more expensive less private
and harder to correct

70
00:04:54,434 --> 00:04:55,495
operationally

71
00:04:56,758 --> 00:05:00,177
This screen shows a separate demonstration batch
that was anchored

72
00:05:00,177 --> 00:05:05,385
earlier Each event has an on-chain verified
status a transaction link

73
00:05:05,385 --> 00:05:09,686
and a downloadable verification proof The
application can recompute

74
00:05:09,686 --> 00:05:14,061
the event hash and compare the Merkle path with
the public root That

75
00:05:14,061 --> 00:05:16,929
gives an external reviewer a way to check
whether the stored event

76
00:05:16,929 --> 00:05:21,096
changed after anchoring The proof checks record
integrity It does

77
00:05:21,096 --> 00:05:24,264
not certify food quality or physical delivery

78
00:05:25,508 --> 00:05:30,037
The impact is practical RescueProof helps small
food businesses and

79
00:05:30,037 --> 00:05:34,502
community organizations coordinate with less
ambiguity A donor can

80
00:05:34,502 --> 00:05:39,285
see what was offered A recipient can see what
was claimed An operator

81
00:05:39,285 --> 00:05:43,417
can see whether the handoff was confirmed or
disputed That shared

82
00:05:43,417 --> 00:05:47,665
history can reduce wasted surplus and make a
local pilot easier to

83
00:05:47,665 --> 00:05:52,167
evaluate The platform records coordination
evidence while leaving

84
00:05:52,167 --> 00:05:56,944
food safety and in-person judgment with the
participating organizations

85
00:05:58,258 --> 00:06:02,289
RescueProof turns a fragmented food rescue
handoff into a workflow

86
00:06:02,289 --> 00:06:06,397
that people can use and reviewers can check This
prototype is open

87
00:06:06,397 --> 00:06:11,435
source testnet-only and based on synthetic data
The next step is a

88
00:06:11,435 --> 00:06:15,676
pilot with accountable food businesses and
community organizations

89
00:06:15,696 --> 00:06:19,726
We are using Web3 where it adds public
accountability while keeping

90
00:06:19,726 --> 00:06:24,872
routine actions familiar and private Thank you
for reviewing RescueProof
